  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday: A quasi-stationary low will continue to \nsit over the region through the Labor Day weekend. It will pump \nmoisture into the region as precipitable water amounts rise from\n0.5-0.6 of an inch on Saturday to 0.8-1.0 by Monday. The \nincreasing moisture will bring a steady stream of clouds and\nshowers across the region. For Saturday, the Cascades have the \nbest chance of receiving a tenth of an inch of rain with around \n30-50 percent probability. It spreads across the Inland \nNorthwest for Sunday. By Monday, the Idaho Panhandle and has the\nbest chance with around 60-80 percent probability. The chances \ndecrease westward with Dayton to Colville being 40 percent and \nthe Central Washington region having 10-20 percent. For the 72 \nhour period, the region as at least a 20 percent probability of \na tenth of an inch of rain. The Central Cascades and Idaho \nPanhandle have a 30 percent probability of receiving half an \ninch.\n\nLate morning, afternoon instability will lead to weak \nthunderstorm chances for the period. Cape values are in the \n300-600 J/kg range with moderate lapse rates support weak \nconvection. The main threat with the thunderstorms will be \nlightning and gusty outflow winds. The best chance for \nthunderstorms will be over the Cascades on Saturday, across the \nInland Northwest on Sunday, and the Idaho Panhandle on Monday. \nWinds will generally be light outside of the thunderstorms. \nMonday afternoon will be breezy with gusts near 25 mph across \nthe Basin.\n\nThe moisture and clouds will make the weekend a mild one. Highs\nwill be in 70s to low 80s. Lows will also be mild with a range \nof 40s to low 50s. \n\nTuesday through Friday: The ensembles are in decent agreement \nfor the extended. A zonal pattern sets up over the region as the\nLow exits. It will lead to dry, warming trend through the end \nof the week. Precip chances will be less than 10 percent through\nThursday. Another trough will develop Friday as remnants of \ntropical system begin to press moisture into the Pacific \nNorthwest. It is leading to increasing precip chances going into\nthe weekend. It is too early to have much confidence in the \nupcoming weekend weather but something to keep on the radar. \nHighs for the latter half of the week will be in the 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 40s and 50s. /JDC",
